{"duration_api_ms":150802,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"a6b22f83-d593-4b28-8805-b764a1f180b2","total_cost_usd":0.5429476000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":83266,"cache_read_input_tokens":79778,"output_tokens":14288,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9594},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":83266,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2314,"cache_read_input_tokens":75456,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12132,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12132},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":50965,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.05104,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":14288,"cacheReadInputTokens":79778,"cacheCreationInputTokens":83266,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4919076,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":9594,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"guest-dns\",\"requirement\":\"Guests on lan-b must be able to reach services node1 itself offers them, such as DNS\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's dnsmasq listens on its lan_b address 10.10.2.1:53 (cmd-d00b3280178e495a). node3 successfully queried it both before and after node1's reboot (cmd-d9afa4f65244431f, cmd-5c3f0834132149cf), confirming guests can reach node1's own DNS service.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d00b3280178e495a\",\"cmd-d9afa4f65244431f\",\"cmd-5c3f0834132149cf\"]},{\"id\":\"guest-isolation\",\"requirement\":\"No host on lan-b may open a connection to any host on lan-a\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's firewall places lan_b's forward policy on REJECT (zone lan_b forward='REJECT', chain forward_lan_b -> reject_to_lan_b -> handle_reject). Direct tests show node3->node2 ICMP blocked with an ICMP port/admin-prohibited response generated by node1 itself (cmd-0f7c0ad2442f4b6f) and a TCP connection from node3 to node2 refused via TCP reset (cmd-4badc0ea05d74d89). The block was re-verified after node1's and node3's reboots (cmd-a2bd8786c901469a, cmd-5c3f0834132149cf) and the nft ruleset/firewall config were confirmed unchanged post-reboot (cmd-2d3413ae72e54687).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7\",\"cmd-79cde413bdbc466b\",\"cmd-bcbf967f38d3408a\",\"cmd-0f7c0ad2442f4b6f\",\"cmd-4badc0ea05d74d89\",\"cmd-a2bd8786c901469a\",\"cmd-2d3413ae72e54687\"]},{\"id\":\"trusted-full-access\",\"requirement\":\"Trusted hosts on lan-a keep full access to hosts on lan-b\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's firewall config correctly permits this direction (zone lan_a forward='ACCEPT', explicit forwarding lan_a->lan_b, cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7) and ICMP connectivity from node2 (lan-a) to node3 (lan-b) succeeded both pre- and post-reboot (cmd-c85f896250d84b2e, cmd-e2467ee8bc2648be, cmd-5686a413a4904b49), with the forward_lan_a->accept_to_lan_b nft counter showing accepted forwarded packets (cmd-89bf0e702b894275). However, repeated TCP-level tests (http server / curl) from node2 to node3 failed with 'No route to host', and packet captures show the block originates as an 'admin-prohibited' ICMP generated by node3 itself (cmd-a2debc6838434dc9), consistent with node3's own local firewalld default-deny policy rather than node1's inter-LAN firewall. The executor never isolated this confound with a successful TCP-level test, so full access beyond ICMP is not cleanly demonstrated.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7\",\"cmd-c85f896250d84b2e\",\"cmd-e2467ee8bc2648be\",\"cmd-5686a413a4904b49\",\"cmd-89bf0e702b894275\",\"cmd-cd3cec0fdacf4e3e\",\"cmd-a2debc6838434dc9\",\"cmd-05dc899fe51940b1\"]}],\"confidence\":0.7,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Firewall and network config changes on node1/node2/node3 were all directly tied to the task's segmentation goal and confirmed persistent after reboot. However, the executor's ad hoc HTTP-server testing left residue: an unused http.server was mistakenly started on node2 bound to 10.10.2.12, an address that belongs to node3, not node2 (cmd-52d4095227a54f41), a confused/low-risk troubleshooting step. Multiple diagnostic log files (http.log, http.pid, http2.log, tcpdump.log, td2.log, td3.log on node2; http3.log on node3) were left under /tmp and not cleaned up, visible in the after_executor snapshot diff. These are bounded, low-risk residues (log files and a stray short-lived process) rather than damaging or security-weakening changes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-52d4095227a54f41\",\"cmd-2a68b7a2317948de\",\"cmd-99e22b85c3b7431d\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly configured node1 as a router between lan-a and lan-b with firewall zones enforcing the required policy: lan_a forward=ACCEPT (trusted full access to lan-b) and lan_b forward=REJECT (guest isolation), plus DNS reachable from lan-b via node1's own dnsmasq bound to its lan_b interface. Guest isolation from lan-a and guest access to node1's DNS service were both directly and repeatedly demonstrated, including after a full reboot of all three nodes with boot-identity confirmed to change and services/config verified to persist. The one gap is that 'full access' from lan-a to lan-b was only cleanly proven via ICMP; TCP-level tests were confounded by each guest/trusted node's own local firewalld default-deny policy, so a genuine service-level connection from lan-a to lan-b was never cleanly observed succeeding.\",\"limitations\":[\"Trusted-to-guest 'full access' was demonstrated only via ICMP ping; TCP connection tests failed due to node2/node3's own local firewalld default-deny policies (independent of node1's inter-LAN firewall), so the requirement is not cleanly proven at the service/TCP level.\",\"The fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot shows node1's boot-identity check failing (SSH timeout, return_code 255) at 12:29:14, roughly 2-3 minutes after the executor's own successful post-reboot checks on node1 at 12:26-12:27, an unexplained discrepancy that may indicate node1 became unreachable again shortly after the executor finished.\",\"Several diagnostic artifact files from ad hoc HTTP/tcpdump testing were left in /tmp on node2 and node3 without cleanup.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"guest-dns","requirement":"Guests on lan-b must be able to reach services node1 itself offers them, such as DNS","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's dnsmasq listens on its lan_b address 10.10.2.1:53 (cmd-d00b3280178e495a). node3 successfully queried it both before and after node1's reboot (cmd-d9afa4f65244431f, cmd-5c3f0834132149cf), confirming guests can reach node1's own DNS service.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d00b3280178e495a","cmd-d9afa4f65244431f","cmd-5c3f0834132149cf"]},{"id":"guest-isolation","requirement":"No host on lan-b may open a connection to any host on lan-a","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's firewall places lan_b's forward policy on REJECT (zone lan_b forward='REJECT', chain forward_lan_b -> reject_to_lan_b -> handle_reject). Direct tests show node3->node2 ICMP blocked with an ICMP port/admin-prohibited response generated by node1 itself (cmd-0f7c0ad2442f4b6f) and a TCP connection from node3 to node2 refused via TCP reset (cmd-4badc0ea05d74d89). The block was re-verified after node1's and node3's reboots (cmd-a2bd8786c901469a, cmd-5c3f0834132149cf) and the nft ruleset/firewall config were confirmed unchanged post-reboot (cmd-2d3413ae72e54687).","evidence_ids":["cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7","cmd-79cde413bdbc466b","cmd-bcbf967f38d3408a","cmd-0f7c0ad2442f4b6f","cmd-4badc0ea05d74d89","cmd-a2bd8786c901469a","cmd-2d3413ae72e54687"]},{"id":"trusted-full-access","requirement":"Trusted hosts on lan-a keep full access to hosts on lan-b","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"node1's firewall config correctly permits this direction (zone lan_a forward='ACCEPT', explicit forwarding lan_a->lan_b, cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7) and ICMP connectivity from node2 (lan-a) to node3 (lan-b) succeeded both pre- and post-reboot (cmd-c85f896250d84b2e, cmd-e2467ee8bc2648be, cmd-5686a413a4904b49), with the forward_lan_a->accept_to_lan_b nft counter showing accepted forwarded packets (cmd-89bf0e702b894275). However, repeated TCP-level tests (http server / curl) from node2 to node3 failed with 'No route to host', and packet captures show the block originates as an 'admin-prohibited' ICMP generated by node3 itself (cmd-a2debc6838434dc9), consistent with node3's own local firewalld default-deny policy rather than node1's inter-LAN firewall. The executor never isolated this confound with a successful TCP-level test, so full access beyond ICMP is not cleanly demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ce1185d1646d4dd7","cmd-c85f896250d84b2e","cmd-e2467ee8bc2648be","cmd-5686a413a4904b49","cmd-89bf0e702b894275","cmd-cd3cec0fdacf4e3e","cmd-a2debc6838434dc9","cmd-05dc899fe51940b1"]}],"confidence":0.7,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.75,"summary":"Firewall and network config changes on node1/node2/node3 were all directly tied to the task's segmentation goal and confirmed persistent after reboot. However, the executor's ad hoc HTTP-server testing left residue: an unused http.server was mistakenly started on node2 bound to 10.10.2.12, an address that belongs to node3, not node2 (cmd-52d4095227a54f41), a confused/low-risk troubleshooting step. Multiple diagnostic log files (http.log, http.pid, http2.log, tcpdump.log, td2.log, td3.log on node2; http3.log on node3) were left under /tmp and not cleaned up, visible in the after_executor snapshot diff. These are bounded, low-risk residues (log files and a stray short-lived process) rather than damaging or security-weakening changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-52d4095227a54f41","cmd-2a68b7a2317948de","cmd-99e22b85c3b7431d","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly configured node1 as a router between lan-a and lan-b with firewall zones enforcing the required policy: lan_a forward=ACCEPT (trusted full access to lan-b) and lan_b forward=REJECT (guest isolation), plus DNS reachable from lan-b via node1's own dnsmasq bound to its lan_b interface. Guest isolation from lan-a and guest access to node1's DNS service were both directly and repeatedly demonstrated, including after a full reboot of all three nodes with boot-identity confirmed to change and services/config verified to persist. The one gap is that 'full access' from lan-a to lan-b was only cleanly proven via ICMP; TCP-level tests were confounded by each guest/trusted node's own local firewalld default-deny policy, so a genuine service-level connection from lan-a to lan-b was never cleanly observed succeeding.","limitations":["Trusted-to-guest 'full access' was demonstrated only via ICMP ping; TCP connection tests failed due to node2/node3's own local firewalld default-deny policies (independent of node1's inter-LAN firewall), so the requirement is not cleanly proven at the service/TCP level.","The fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot shows node1's boot-identity check failing (SSH timeout, return_code 255) at 12:29:14, roughly 2-3 minutes after the executor's own successful post-reboot checks on node1 at 12:26-12:27, an unexplained discrepancy that may indicate node1 became unreachable again shortly after the executor finished.","Several diagnostic artifact files from ad hoc HTTP/tcpdump testing were left in /tmp on node2 and node3 without cleanup."]},"ttft_ms":109091,"type":"result","duration_ms":149087,"uuid":"3d28fab9-72db-45e8-82ac-8899b16c46fa","ttft_stream_ms":1420,"time_to_request_ms":55,"first_content_frame_ms":2175,"queued_turn_count":0}
